From 4cd1994ace498948d9d9f735e4f82006264af965 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: port <port@localhost>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 09:09:41 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 07/14] core: tessellate DefineShapes on first render, parse
 shape records on demand

Ruffle registered (tessellated) every DefineShape of a SWF while preloading
it, and uploaded the bitmaps of the shapes bitmap fills at the same time. It
also kept the parsed records of every shape for the life of the movie. For
One large AVM1 game (one SWF with every level) that was 4,255
meshes (131 MB of vertex and index buffers), 44 bitmap fill textures (226 MB)
and about 95 MB of shape records, most of it for levels that are not being
played. The game was OOM-killed on 1 GB handhelds.

* Lazy shapes (RUFFLE_LAZY_SHAPES=0 restores the old behaviour): a Graphic
  made from a DefineShape tag no longer has a render handle from preload. It
  is tessellated through the existing TessellationCache the first time it is
  rendered, at the scale it is rendered at, and only then are its fill
  bitmaps uploaded.
* With eager tessellation still on, the preload handle (scale 1) now counts
  as a cache entry within the cache scale range; before, the first render
  tessellated the shape a second time and kept both meshes.
* Lazy shape records (RUFFLE_LAZY_SHAPE_RECORDS=0 restores the old
  behaviour): the DefineShape tag bytes are kept as a SwfSlice of the movie
  data (already in memory), the parsed records are dropped after preload and
  parsed again when the shape is first rendered, hit-tested or read as a
  drawing (Graphics.readGraphicsData). A parse error logs and gives an empty
  shape.

World 2 Level 1 peak on the PC: 813 -> 351 MB (both changes). Rendering and
collisions are unchanged (screenshots and a scripted walk through the level
match the old build). The cost moves to the first time a shape is shown;
Worlds 2 and 3 draw their level art into cache bitmaps while the level loads,
so play is not affected there.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
